## Why I left Omarchy

After using Omarchy for a while and trying to get comfortable with it, I
noticed something I wasn't happy with. It was clean-looking and let me be
free with whatever theme or look I wanted, but I believe it also took away
what makes ricing Linux fun in the first place: figuring out what works,
what you like, and what you don't, through your own trial and error. It came
pre-installed with a lot of apps I didn't use or would have preferred a
different alternative for, which didn't leave much room for that.

Not to mention, the NVIDIA issues I faced kept becoming more and more
noticeable. Ironically, Omarchy ended up feeling bloated, the same complaint
I originally had about Windows, and at times it felt overwhelming, which I
didn't like as someone who wasn't yet used to the Linux space. Using it made
me realise what I actually wanted: something clean and simple to start with,
that I could slowly rice and make more complex over time. That way I could
transition from something easy to understand to a more complex system I had
full control over, instead of starting at "complex" and working backward.

Giving Omarchy the entire 1 TB drive also stopped making sense the more I
thought about it. One of my reasons for installing a separate drive in the
first place was that Windows was running low on space, and handing the whole
second drive to Linux undid that. Between the lost space, the apps I didn't
need, and the NVIDIA friction, I decided to reset and try a more
beginner-friendly distro instead.
